The most important part: "simultaneously"
Suppose:
code = [5,7,1,4]
k = 3

We calculate everything using the original array.
We DON'T do:
5 → 12
then use 12 while calculating the next element

How do we make our index wrap around when it reaches the beginning/end?

That's where the % operator becomes very useful.
For example:
int index = (i + 1) % n;
If you write a sliding-window solution, you'll be moving right many times, and you'll constantly need to check:
if (right == n) {
    right = 0;
}
Instead, Java gives us a cleaner way:
right = (right + 1) % n;

or moving backward, you can use:
right = (right - 1 + n) % n;

//////////code:
class Solution {
    public int[] decrypt(int[] code, int k) {

        int n = code.length;
        int[] result = new int[n];

        if (k == 0) {
            return result;
        }

        int left = 0;

        if (k > 0) {

            while (left < n) {

                int sum = 0;

                for (int right = 0; right < Math.abs(k); right++) {

                    int index = (left + right + 1) % n;
                    sum = sum + code[index];
                }

                result[left] = sum;
                left++;
            }
        }

        if (k < 0) {

            while (left < n) {

                int sum = 0;

                for (int right = 0; right < Math.abs(k); right++) {

                    int index = (left - right - 1 + n) % n;
                    sum = sum + code[index];
                }

                result[left] = sum;
                left++;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
